Tile raster cost instrumentation (WEBKIT_TILECOST_LOG=1, off by default).

Measures what the scroll-degradation ladder is currently blind to: how much a
tile actually COSTS to rasterize. The ladder in CoordinatedBackingStoreProxy
triggers on scroll SPEED, a pure geometry term -- it knows how fast tiles are
being exposed but not what they cost, so it cannot distinguish a raster-bound
page from a cheap one. Measured on build 618, the same WEBKIT_LOWRES_TILE_SCALE
knob is worth -38% p95 frame time on a raster-bound page and +5.7% p95 / +11%
raster CPU on a cheap one, and the current trigger fires on both.

Adds an EWMA of raster nanoseconds per PAINTED pixel, sampled at both raster
sites (the synchronous main-thread paint() and the threaded replay() task, the
latter being the live path on this device: WEBKIT_SKIA_ENABLE_CPU_RENDERING=1
with 2 SkiaCPUWorker threads). Per-painted-pixel rather than per-tile keeps the
statistic independent of tile size and of the low-res scale in force, so a
full-res prediction is nsPerPixel x fullResTileArea and the estimator does not
drift as a controller varies that scale.

Exposed via SkiaPaintingEngine::tileRasterNsPerPixel() /
tileRasterSampleCount() / predictedTileRasterMs() for a subsequent cost-aware
trigger. This patch only MEASURES -- it changes no rendering behaviour and no
decision. WEBKIT_TILECOST_LOG=1 logs one throttled line per 32 tiles.
Applies after webkit-lowres-tiles-during-scroll-env.patch and
webkit-lowres-tiles-cpu-path.patch (contexts overlap in SkiaPaintingEngine.cpp).

